blocks:
  - type: metin
    metin: |-
      A divorce, a loss, a new sibling, an argument that never ends. When a difficult period begins, parents often carry two things at once: what they are going through themselves, and the worry about how their child is taking it. Sleep is disrupted, school mornings get harder, tension between siblings rises — and "what should I do?" is asked again every day.

      This article covers how parent counselling works during such periods and when it may help to get in touch. For the general frame, see [what parent counselling is](/en/blog/parent-counselling.html).
  - type: metin
    baslik: Why start with the parent?
    metin: |-
      The parent is the person who builds a child's daily life. What changes at home during a difficult period — who speaks when, which rule loosens, which subject is never raised — is among the main things that shape how a child lives through it.

      In parent counselling a second question sits alongside "why is this child behaving like this?": "which loop at home keeps this behaviour going?" That second question blames no one; it makes visible what could change at home.
  - type: bilgi-kutusu
    ton: yesil
    baslik: Parent counselling does not look for someone to blame
    metin: The psychologist does not take sides between parents. The aim is not to decide who is right but to make the patterns of communication and interaction at home visible, and to build ground where both parents can see what the child needs.
  - type: kart-yigini
    baslik: When can parent counselling help?
    kartlar:
      - baslik: Recurring conflict and communication breakdown
        metin: When the same argument turns every day and the feeling "we can no longer talk" has taken hold, the sessions focus on how the argument starts and how it closes, and on building a different sequence at home.
      - baslik: Losing touch with a teenager
        metin: Faced with school refusal, withdrawal, anger or risky behaviour, parents often feel caught between setting boundaries and giving room. Even if the young person does not want to attend, work with the parent can change how communication runs at home.
      - baslik: Separation or divorce
        metin: How to tell the child, what routine to build across two homes, and how parents can keep communicating without putting the child in the middle — these are what the sessions cover in this period.
      - baslik: Loss or serious illness
        metin: The loss or serious illness of a family member affects each child differently depending on their age. Together we look at what to tell the child, how to answer their questions, and how grief can be spoken about at home.
      - baslik: A new member or a major life transition
        metin: A new baby, a house move, a change of school, a new partner joining the family — these transitions reorganise the balance at home. We talk about what a parent can pay attention to while the child adjusts.
  - type: metin
    baslik: Should the child attend?
    metin: |-
      It depends on the situation. In parent counselling a good share of the sessions run with the parent alone; with younger children especially, most of the change happens through the approach at home.

      If we find together that the child needs their own space for their emotional world, [child psychology](/en/services/child-psychology.html) or [adolescent psychology](/en/services/adolescent-psychology.html) sessions can run alongside the work with the parent, depending on age. No child is pushed into a session they are not ready for.
  - type: metin
    baslik: 'If you were searching for a "family psychologist"'
    metin: |-
      Parents looking for help with a difficulty at home often search for a "family psychologist" or "family counsellor". What they usually want is to understand their child's behaviour and to know what to do at home.

      At our centre we meet that need through [parent counselling](/en/services/parent-counselling.html). The focus is the sessions with the parent and the relationship between parent and child; where needed, sessions with the child or teenager accompany it.
  - type: metin
    baslik: What happens in the first session?
    metin: |-
      In the [first session](/en/blog/before-starting-therapy.html) the psychologist gets to know you, your child and the change your family is going through. What started when, what has been tried at home and what made things harder are discussed. Both parents can come, or one on their own. The session is for assessment; nobody is judged.

      How long the process runs varies with what the family needs; we do not put a number on it in advance. Sessions last 50 minutes and usually take place weekly or every two weeks.
